323-Starter-2-Q10,word_problem,Order these temperatures from least to greatest. Then name the place with the warmest temperature.,Maths,6,Integers & Operations,Number Theory,Students can compare and order positive and negative integers,Applying,easy,1,"Generated 1 beginner Grade 6 word problem on comparing and ordering positive and negative integers, using a fresh scenario and avoiding recent standout values.","{""scenario"":""During a science trip, students recorded the temperatures at four places in a nature center: the reptile room was 12°C, the penguin area was -6°C, the lobby was 1°C, and the ice lab was -15°C."",""solutionSteps"":[""List the temperatures: 12, -6, 1, and -15."",""Negative integers are less than positive integers."",""Among the negative integers, -15 is less than -6."",""Among the positive integers, 1 is less than 12."",""So from least to greatest, the temperatures are -15, -6, 1, 12."",""The greatest temperature is 12°C, so the reptile room is the warmest place.""],""finalAnswer"":""From least to greatest: -15°C, -6°C, 1°C, 12°C. The warmest place is the reptile room."",""hints"":[""Start by putting the negative temperatures before the positive temperatures."",""For negative integers, the number farther left on the number line is smaller."",""After ordering the numbers, identify the greatest temperature.""],""requiresCalculation"":false,""scoringGuidance"":""Award full marks for correctly ordering all four integers from least to greatest and correctly naming the reptile room as the warmest place. Give partial credit if the student correctly places negative numbers before positive numbers but makes one ordering error, or if the order is correct but the place name is missing."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Integers & Operations"",""subtopic"":""Number Theory"",""learning_objective"":""Students can compare and order positive and negative integers"",""focus"":null}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""que