(c) the constant",Maths,6,Introduction to Algebra,Patterns & Algebra,"Students can identify variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions",Understanding,medium,5,"Generated 1 Grade 6 word problem on identifying variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions, using a fresh scenario and non-repeated standout values.","{""scenario"":""A school art club sells greeting cards to raise money. The total money collected is represented by the expression 8c + 11, where c is the number of card packs sold."",""solutionSteps"":[""Look at the expression 8c + 11."",""The letter c can change depending on how many card packs are sold, so c is the variable."",""The number multiplying c is 8, so 8 is the coefficient of c."",""The number added on its own is 11, so 11 is the constant.""],""finalAnswer"":""(a) c\n(b) 8\n(c) 11"",""hints"":[""A variable is usually shown by a letter."",""A coefficient is the number attached to the variable."",""A constant is the number with no variable.""],""requiresCalculation"":false,""scoringGuidance"":""Award 1 mark each for correctly identifying the variable as c, the coefficient as 8, and the constant as 11. Give partial credit if only one or two parts are correct. If a student confuses coefficient and constant, award marks only for the correctly identified parts."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learning_objective"":""Students can identify variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions"",""focus"":null}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""queueId"":""326-Starter-6-Q10"",""topicId"":""326"",""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learningObjective"":""Students can identify variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions"",""levelTag"":""Intermediate"",""topicFamily"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""grade"":6,""subject"":""Maths"",""questionCount"":1,""testType"":""Starter"",""proficiencyLevel"":""Master"",""questionMedium"":""WP"",""recentQuestions"":[""In the expression 4p + 9q - 21, which statement is correct?"",""In the expression 7y + 10, the coefficient of y is 7."",""In the expression 7y + 10, the variable is ___."",""In the expression 5k - 2, the coefficient of k is ___."",""Match each part of the expression 7x + 2y + 10 to its correct description."",""Drag each part of the expression 7x - 10 + 5y into the correct box."",""Look at the expression 7y + 10 - 2z.\nIdentify:\n(a) one variable\n(b) the constant\n(c) the coefficient of z"",""In the expression 5b + 17, identify:\n(a) the variable\n(b) the coefficient of b\n(c) the constant""],""recentStandoutValues"":[""14"",""12"",""15"",""7"",""6"",""5"",""16"",""-16"",""4"",""9"",""21"",""10"",""-2"",""3"",""-10"",""17""],""avoidNumberReuse"":true,""calibrationExamples"":[{""difficultyBand"":""Basic"",""question"":""In the expression 4x + 7, which part is the constant?\nA) 4   B) x   C) 7   D) 4x"",""answer"":""C) 7"",""markingNote"":""Accept C only. If student selects 4, they are confusing coefficient with constant - note for clarification.""},{""difficultyBand"":""Pro"",""question"":""Look at the expression: 3a – 5b + 9.\nIdentify: (a) one variable, (b) the constant, (c) the coefficient of b."",""answer"":""(a) a or b   (b) 9   (c) –5 (or 5)"",""markingNote"":""Each part marked independently. Accept –5 or 5 for coefficient of b - note if student ignores the negative sign.""}],""questionType"":""word_problem""},""batchRequest"":{""topicId"":""326"",""learningObjective"":""Students can identify variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions"",""testType"":""Starter"",""questionMedium"":""WP"",""queueIds"":[""326-Starter-6-Q10""]},""spec"":{""summary"":""Generated 1 Grade 6 word problem on identifying variables, constants, and coefficients in algebraic expressions, using a fresh scenario and non-repeated standout values.""},""deterministicValidation"":{""isValid"":true,""issues"":[]}}"
326-Starter-8-Q1,mcq,"A taxi driver charges ₹14 for each airport trip. In one day, he earns ₹154 from airport trips. If t is the number of trips, which equation represents this situation?",Maths,6,Introduction to Algebra,Patterns & Algebra,Students can write equations from real-life situations,Understanding,easy,1,"3 classroom-ready MCQs on writing equations from real-life situations in Introduction to Algebra, with varied contexts and numbers, and a mix of easy to hard difficulty.","{""options"":[{""text"":""14 + t = 154"",""correct"":false},{""text"":""14t = 154"",""correct"":true},{""text"":""154t = 14"",""correct"":false},{""text"":""t - 14 = 154"",""correct"":false}],""explanation"":""Since each trip costs ₹14, total earnings are found by multiplying 14 by the number of trips: 14t = 154."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learning_objective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""focus"":""multiplication equation from equal groups""}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""queueId"":""326-Starter-8-Q1"",""topicId"":""326"",""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learningObjective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""levelTag"":""Intermediate"",""topicFamily"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""grade"":6,""subject"":""Maths"",""questionCount"":1,""testType"":""Starter"",""proficiencyLevel"":""Basic"",""questionMedium"":""MCQ"",""recentQuestions"":[""Leela saves the same amount each week. After 9 weeks, she has saved ₹72. Which equation shows this situation if s is the amount she saves each week?"",""A fruit seller packs oranges in bags of 6. After adding 11 loose oranges, there are 47 oranges altogether. If b is the number of bags, the equation 6b + 11 = 47 represents this situation."",""A music club charges ₹18 for each badge. If Sohan buys some badges and pays ₹126 in total, the equation is ___."",""Lina had some marbles. After she won 16 more marbles, she had 44 marbles altogether, so the equation is ___."",""Match each real-life situation to the equation that represents it."",""Match each real-life situation to the equation that represents it."",""A bakery packs 8 muffins in each box. Then 10 extra muffins are placed on a tray, making 74 muffins altogether. If b is the number of boxes, write an equation to represent this situation."",""If c is the number of comic books Aarav rented, write an equation to represent this situation. Then find how many comic books he rented.""],""recentStandoutValues"":[""47"",""9"",""72"",""18"",""126"",""16"",""44"",""24"",""4"",""41"",""61"",""10"",""74"",""98"",""80"",""5""],""avoidNumberReuse"":true,""calibrationExamples"":[{""difficultyBand"":""Basic"",""question"":""Tickets to a show cost ₹60 each. Ravi buys some tickets and pays ₹300 total. Which equation fits?\nA) 60 + t = 300   B) 60t = 300   C) t ÷ 60 = 300   D) t – 60 = 300"",""answer"":""B) 60t = 300"",""markingNote"":""Accept B. If student selects A, they are confusing multiplication with addition structure - use to reteach.""},{""difficultyBand"":""Pro"",""question"":""A shopkeeper has some boxes. After receiving 15 more, he has 48 boxes in total. Write an equation and find the number of boxes he started with."",""answer"":""b + 15 = 48 → b = 33"",""markingNote"":""Full marks for correct equation AND solution. Award partial marks for correct equation with arithmetic error in solving.""}],""questionType"":""mcq""},""batchRequest"":{""topicId"":""326"",""learningObjective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""testType"":""Starter"",""questionMedium"":""MCQ"",""queueIds"":[""326-Starter-8-Q1"",""326-Starter-8-Q2"",""326-Starter-8-Q8""]},""spec"":{""summary"":""3 classroom-ready MCQs on writing equations from real-life situations in Introduction to Algebra, with varied contexts and numbers, and a mix of easy to hard difficulty.""},""deterministicValidation"":{""isValid"":true,""issues"":[]}}"
326-Starter-8-Q2,mcq,"A library has some new books on a shelf. After 27 more books are added, there are 83 books altogether. If b is the number of books that were there at first, which equation shows this situation?",Maths,6,Introduction to Algebra,Patterns & Algebra,Students can write equations from real-life situations,Applying,medium,3,"3 classroom-ready MCQs on writing equations from real-life situations in Introduction to Algebra, with varied contexts and numbers, and a mix of easy to hard difficulty.","{""options"":[{""text"":""27b = 83"",""correct"":false},{""text"":""b - 27 = 83"",""correct"":false},{""text"":""b + 27 = 83"",""correct"":true},{""text"":""83 + b = 27"",""correct"":false}],""explanation"":""Start with b books, then add 27 more to get 83, so the equation is b + 27 = 83."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learning_objective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""focus"":""addition equation from a starting amount""}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""queueId"":""326-Starter-8-Q2"",""topicId"":""326"",""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learningObjective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""levelTag"":""Intermediate"",""topicFamily"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""grade"":6,""subject"":""Maths"",""questionCount"":1,""testType"":""Starter"",""proficiencyLevel"":""Basic"",""questionMedium"":""MCQ"",""recentQuestions"":[""Leela saves the same amount each week. After 9 weeks, she has saved ₹72. Which equation shows this situation if s is the amount she saves each week?"",""A fruit seller packs oranges in bags of 6. After adding 11 loose oranges, there are 47 oranges altogether. If b is the number of bags, the equation 6b + 11 = 47 represents this situation."",""A music club charges ₹18 for each badge. If Sohan buys some badges and pays ₹126 in total, the equation i