322-Starter-12-Q10,word_problem,"Without working out the exact total, estimate how many tickets the museum will print altogether. Explain how you rounded the numbers.",Maths,6,Advanced Arithmetic,Number Operations,Students can use rounding and approximation to estimate answers,Applying,medium,5,"Generated 1 Grade 6 word problem on rounding and approximation to estimate answers, aligned to Advanced Arithmetic > Number Operations.","{""scenario"":""A science museum is printing 347 tickets for each of 58 school groups visiting this month."",""solutionSteps"":[""Round 347 to a sensible number: 350 or 300 are both reasonable; use 350 for a closer estimate."",""Round 58 to 60."",""Multiply the rounded numbers: 350 × 60 = 21,000."",""So the total number of tickets is about 21,000.""],""finalAnswer"":""About 21,000 tickets."",""hints"":[""Think about which nearby numbers are easy to multiply mentally."",""Round both numbers to values that keep the estimate sensible."",""After rounding, multiply the new numbers.""],""requiresCalculation"":true,""scoringGuidance"":""Award full marks for a reasonable estimate with a clear rounding explanation, such as 347 → 350 and 58 → 60, followed by 350 × 60 = 21,000. Award most marks if the student uses a different but sensible rounding strategy, such as 300 × 60 = 18,000, and explains it clearly. Award partial marks for correct rounding without completing the multiplication, or for a correct multiplication method after reasonable rounded values. Do not require the exact product."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Advanced Arithmetic"",""subtopic"":""Number Operations"",""learning_objective"":""Students can use rounding and approximation to estimate answers"",""focus"":""estimation by rounding before multiplication""}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""queueId"":""322-Starter-12-Q10"",""topicId"":""322"",""topic"":""Advanced Arithmetic"",""subtopic"":""Number Operations"",""learningObjective"":""St