326-Pulse-8-Q5,word_problem,Write an equation to represent this situation if the number of notebooks is n. Then find how many notebooks Leena buys.,Maths,6,Introduction to Algebra,Patterns & Algebra,Students can write equations from real-life situations,Applying,medium,3,Generated 1 Grade 6 word problem on writing and solving an equation from a real-life situation in Patterns & Algebra.,"{""scenario"":""Leena is buying notebooks for her class project. Each notebook costs ₹14. She also buys 1 folder for ₹9. The total amount she spends is ₹93."",""solutionSteps"":[""Let n be the number of notebooks."",""Each notebook costs ₹14, so the cost of the notebooks is 14n."",""The folder costs ₹9, and the total spent is ₹93."",""So the equation is 14n + 9 = 93."",""Subtract 9 from both sides: 14n = 84."",""Divide both sides by 14: n = 6.""],""finalAnswer"":""Equation: 14n + 9 = 93; Leena buys 6 notebooks."",""hints"":[""Use a variable for the number of notebooks."",""Multiply the cost of 1 notebook by the number of notebooks."",""Do not forget to add the cost of the folder.""],""requiresCalculation"":true,""scoringGuidance"":""Award full marks for writing a correct equation, 14n + 9 = 93, and correctly solving it to get n = 6. Award partial credit if the equation structure is correct but there is an arithmetic mistake while solving. If the student writes only the answer without an equation, award limited credit."",""metadata"":{""subject"":""Maths"",""grade"":6,""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learning_objective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""focus"":null}}","{""source"":""grade6-workbook"",""request"":{""queueId"":""326-Pulse-8-Q5"",""topicId"":""326"",""topic"":""Introduction to Algebra"",""subtopic"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""learningObjective"":""Students can write equations from real-life situations"",""levelTag"":""Intermediate"",""topicFamily"":""Patterns & Algebra"",""grade"":6,""subject"":""Maths"",""questionCount"":1,""testType"":""Pulse"",""proficiencyLevel"":""Pro"",""questionMedium"":""WP"",""recentQuestions"":[""Write an equation to show this situation, and find how much money Meera had at first."",""A school library packed some books into equal stacks. Each stack has 8 books. After making the stacks, 6 extra books were added, and there were 54 books in all. Write an equation to show this situation, and find how many stacks were made."",""A movie ticket costs ₹35. If Ayaan spends ₹245 in total, the equation that shows the number of tickets t is 35t = ___."",""A snack box costs ₹12 each. Rohan buys some snack boxes and spends ₹96 altogether. Which equation represents this situation if the number of snack boxes is s?"",""Drag each part to build the equation that matches this situation: A taxi charges a fixed booking fee of ₹12 and ₹7 for each kilometre traveled. The total fare is ₹68. Match each expression to its place in the equation."",""A fruit seller packs oranges in bags with 7 oranges in each bag. After adding 11 extra oranges, there are 60 oranges altogether. Write an equation to show this situation."",""Write an equation to represent this situation, and find how many tickets Arjun bought."",""A fruit seller packs oranges into bags with 7 oranges in each bag. Then 11 extra oranges are placed outside the bags. There are 81 oranges in all. Write an equation to represent this situation, and find how many bags were packed.""],""recentStandoutValues"":[""48"",""35"",""245"",""210"",""280"",""6"",""12"",""96"",""7"",""68"",""11"",""60"",""8"",""81"",""70"",""10""],""avoidNumberReuse"":true,""calibrationExamples"":[{""difficultyBand"":""Basic"",""question"":""Tickets to a show cost ₹60 each. Ravi buys some tickets and pays ₹300 total. Which equation fits?\nA) 60 + t = 300   B) 60t = 300   C) t ÷ 60 = 300   D) t – 60 = 300"",""answer"":""B) 60t = 300"",""markingNote"":""Accept B. If student selects A, they are confusing multiplication with addition struc